register is a storage class specifier that was originally used to suggest that a variable should be stored in a CPU register instead of RAM.

A register is:
	Very small storage
	Inside the CPU
	Extremely fast (1 clock access)
	Used for calculations

Important: In Modern C++ register Does Nothing
	Since C++11, register is deprecated.
	Since C++17, it is removed.

Why?
Because:
	Modern compilers are WAY smarter than humans.
	The compiler automatically decides:
	Which variables go in registers
	Which go in memory
	When to optimize



register was a storage class specifier suggesting that a variable be stored in a CPU register for faster access. It is deprecated and removed in modern C++ because compilers automatically optimize register allocation.